  "full_response": "There are several main types of software testing, each designed to identify specific kinds of issues and used at various stages of the software development lifecycle. Here's an overview of the most common types of software testing and the appropriate scenarios for their use:\n\n1. Unit Testing:\n   - Definition: Testing individual components (units) of a software application.\n   - Purpose: To validate that each unit of the software performs as designed.\n   - Ideal Time: During the development phase, right after the code for a component is written.\n\n2. Integration Testing:\n   - Definition: Testing the interaction between integrated units/software components.\n   - Purpose: To ensure that the units work together when combined.\n   - Ideal Time: After unit testing and before system testing.\n\n3. Functional Testing:\n   - Definition: Testing the software against its functional requirements.\n   - Purpose: To verify that the application works as expected based on its functional requirements.\n   - Ideal Time: Can be conducted after unit and integration testing, during later stages of development and after system testing.\n\n4. System Testing:\n   - Definition: Testing the entire system as a whole to evaluate its compliance with specified requirements.\n   - Purpose: To ensure that the system meets the overall expectations and that all components work together properly.\n   - Ideal Time: After integration testing, before installation.\n\n5. Acceptance Testing:\n   - Definition: Determining whether the system meets the acceptance criteria and is ready for deployment to the end-users.\n   - Purpose: To validate the end-to-end business flow and that the software fulfills the user's needs.\n   - Ideal Time: After system testing and before deployment to customers or end-users.\n\n6. Performance Testing:\n   - Definition: Evaluating the responsiveness, stability, reliability, and scalability of a system under a given workload.\n   - Purpose: To ensure that the system performs well under anticipated workloads and usage conditions.\n   - Ideal Time: Announced performance testing could occur after system testing, but it should be incorporated throughout development.\n\n7. Usability Testing:\n   - Definition: Assessing the user-friendliness of the software by watching real users attempt to complete tasks.\n   - Purpose: To determine whether the software is easy to use and intuitive for the intended audience.\n   - Ideal Time: Before acceptance testing but ideally at the very end of the development cycle or even during ongoing development, in an iterative manner.\n\n8. Security Testing:\n   - Definition: Evaluating the software for vulnerabilities and threats.\n   - Purpose: To protect against unauthorized access, data breaches, and other threats.\n",
